:::oak-task {id="task-a"}

:::oak-heading {id="task-a-heading" level="2"}
Task A: Defining specific heat capacity
:::

:::oak-question {id="question-1" number="1"}
A pupil warms 0.2 kg of a liquid for 5 minutes with a heater and its temperature rises by 30ºC. They then use the same heater to heat a 0.6 kg sample of the liquid for 30 minutes.

a) State the expected temperature rise and explain your answer.

b) Suggest why the measured temperature rise may not match your answer to part a.

:::oak-answer-space {id="question-1-response" kind="lines" lines="8"}
:::
:::

:::oak-question {id="question-2" number="2"}
Complete the table by calculating the energy required to heat the materials.

:::oak-table {id="energy-to-heat-materials"}
material | mass (kg) | start temp (ºC) | end temp (ºC) | SHC (J/kg ºC) | energy (kJ)
water | 1.5 | 20 | 90 | 4200 | ?
lead | 1.4 | 25 | 200 | 126 | ?
brass | 0.60 | –10 | 50 | 380 | ?
:::
:::
:::

:::oak-task {id="task-b"}

:::oak-heading {id="task-b-heading" level="2"}
Task B: Specific heat capacity calculations
:::

:::oak-question {id="question-3" number="1"}
A kettle provides 8 000 J of energy per second to 1.5 kg of water for one minute. The water starts at a temperature of 20ºC.

Specific heat capacity for water = 4200 J/kg ºC.

a) Calculate the energy provided to the water.

b) Calculate the final temperature of the water.

:::oak-answer-space {id="question-3-response" kind="lines" lines="8"}
:::
:::

:::oak-question {id="question-4" number="2"}
A physicist tests sample blocks of metal by providing them all with 8000 J of energy with a heater. Use the data in the table to calculate the specific heat capacity for each metal.

:::oak-table {id="metal-specific-heat-capacities"}
metal | mass (kg) | start temp (ºC) | end temp (ºC) | SHC (J/kg ºC)
titanium | 0.50 | 20 | 50 | ?
silver | 1.40 | 22 | 47 | ?
gold | 0.20 | 25 | 326 | ?
:::
:::
:::

:::oak-task {id="task-c"}

:::oak-heading {id="task-c-heading" level="2"}
Task C: Solving SHC problems
:::

:::oak-question {id="question-5" number="1"}
An electric heater increases the temperature of a 0.50 kg sample of oil by 40ºC in five minutes. The oil has a specific heat capacity of 2800 J/kg ºC.

Calculate the energy provided by the heater per second.

:::oak-answer-space {id="question-5-response" kind="lines" lines="6"}
:::
:::

:::oak-question {id="question-6" number="2"}
A glass contains 0.10 kg of water. A chilled stone cube of mass 50 g is placed in it and the temperature of the water decreases by 2.0ºC while the stone’s temperature increases by 10ºC. The specific heat capacity of water is 4200 J/kg ºC.

a) Calculate the internal energy change for the water.

b) Calculate the specific heat capacity for the stone.

:::oak-answer-space {id="question-6-response" kind="lines" lines="8"}
:::
